A||N||66||Zorba||Has any  V8 Fiero Toasted A Viper? (stock)||||1||
Z||000000||Zorba||11-21-2000||03:24 PM||||I am wondering if anyone here with a v8 set up has toasted a stock viper. I know you can't toasted a modified one. But dollar versus dollar, I would be curious to know.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/racer.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||11-21-2000||03:57 PM||||dollar for dollar (IE, investing the value of a viper into a fiero) you should be able to take one, but it would take a lot of work and well, how much of your fiero would still be fiero?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Zorba||11-21-2000||04:40 PM||||It looks like the viper is a bit heavier than the v8 fiero <BR>with a Curb weight of 3500 lbs.<BR>It's also producing MORE power with less work with a<BR>8.0-liter V-10, 400 hp; 6-sp man. I assume its torque is around the 400 ft pounds as well. Given the weight to power ratio, it would probably take a 400hp fiero, geared correctly, to take on a stock viper. Assuming equally skilled drivers. I think more than power, perhaps the gear ratios (4-5 speed fiero tranny) versus the strong viper tranny. The viper has an additional<BR>2.3Liters, 2 additional cylinders, decent traction, light weight and all that torque.<BR>I would say it would take a real souped fiero to beat it.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||11-21-2000||04:46 PM||||A v8 on nitrous would do it. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Zorba||11-21-2000||04:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88formula:<BR><B>A v8 on nitrous would do it. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>For what I have seen on the net, the fastest viper is 11.22 sec 1/4 mile. That's the one which is heavily modified and costs over $150,000. A regular, stock viper is a mid 13's 1/4 mile car which is not that impressive. I have heard of v8 fieros doing 12's, dollar for dollar, unless you like the viper looks, the fiero can be faster than a stock viper. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||zeusrekning||11-21-2000||05:08 PM||||There was a blue viper at our local 1/8 mile strip and it sucked ass.<BR>Ran like a mid to high ten.<BR>I gues he wasn't trying but i felt bad seeing it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Zorba||11-21-2000||05:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by zeusrekning:<BR><B>There was a blue viper at our local 1/8 mile strip and it sucked ass.<BR>Ran like a mid to high ten.<BR>I gues he wasn't trying but i felt bad seeing it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I have seen the same happen here. A blue viper thought they were fast and the best time he could do ( he only ran twice) was 14's! At 4,000 ft. The worst thing you can do is shell out $40,000-$75,000+ on a car because you believed all the magazine reviews that you are driving a super fast car. Then you go to the drag strip and get beat by a $10,000 pinto with slicks and a modified motor! The fastest street cars are not necessarily the most expensive ones.<BR>I would much rather have a nicely set up v8 fiero doing 12's than a nice looking NSX doing 14's. Anyway. They just want to sell you a car, right?<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000007||JohnnyK||11-21-2000||05:47 PM||||Vipers are cranking out 450hp, not 400.. Stick that in your pipe and smoke it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||GT Bastard||11-21-2000||06:26 PM||||Who needs a V8 to smoke a Viper if it's running 13's?||||reg||1
Z||000009||webbee||11-21-2000||06:47 PM||||"The fastest street cars are not necessarily the most expensive ones."<P>That is the essence of hot rodding! Now let's get out there and whip some Viper a$$.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||BBBAD GT||11-23-2000||11:38 AM||||Lets not forget about driver error.  You will see the slow Vipers going 114+ mph though in the 1/4, which means the HP is there, just not putting it to the ground too well.  Stock Vipers should manage mid to low 12's with a good driver. <P>I wouldn't be afraid of any Viper (modified or not) in my 5.0 on the street or strip (total investment probably 20+k).    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">    <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Xtreme Fiero's||11-23-2000||12:52 PM||||1st a stock viper sould run mid-low 12's like BBBAD GT said.<P>2nd Vipers have no ass above 100mph I have paced one (98 w/ a targa roof) in my truck though he kicked my ass all over the road we found our ourselves crusing 130mph+ on the local high way and he  could not pull a way that fast up hills he would even pop in down into 5th I could hear the rpms jump granted if he would have stayed in 5th he could have pulled away but that 5th at 130+ in a viperare higher revs than the avrage viper owner whant to put thier $75k car through. most 6-speed cars seem to have the same problem due to the huge gap from 5th to 6th the 5th puts them over ther peak rpm and 6th drops the rpm to low while im just crusing in my "Ricers" 5500 rpm sweet spot<P><BR>Tom D<BR>1995 Nissan D21 Hardbody<BR>3.0 V6 5-speed<BR>13.9 @ 100 mph (lightly masaged)||||reg||1
Z||000012||Zorba||11-23-2000||04:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Xtreme Fiero's:<BR><B>1st a stock viper sould run mid-low 12's like BBBAD GT said.<P> )</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I don't know where you are getting your status but vipers are 13 second cars in stock form and 11.22 second for the henessey 650hp monster. So...there are no vipers doing 12's at the track and i have checked a few viper sites and no one mentions 12's for a stock roadster or coupe.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Fieroboy1||11-23-2000||07:50 PM||||Well i can vouch for bbbaad gt on this one.  I saw a stock (as far as i know) it looked fresh out of the showroom viper RT with STREET tires rip off consistant 12.90's It was quite a sight.  Island Dragway NJ.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Shaun41178||11-23-2000||09:30 PM||||The first few years of Vipers had 400 hp.  Now all of them from the factory have 450 hp and as much torque.<P>I have seen Vipers at the track rip off mid 12's at the track and that is with tearing up their street tires and getting about a 2.3 60' time.  Mph was over 110 through the traps.  That is moving pretty good.  I dont' ever remember reading any magazines that have tested a Viper in the 13's either.<P>Oh yea I also saw a Viper making trial passes when I was at the Sport Compact Nationals this year.  I believe it was an auto tranny Viper as well as I don't remember hearing the car shift at all like a manual.  Anyways it was a couple of kids in the car and they were easing it out of the whole so much I could have ran out faster with my own two feet.  Anyways these kids still clocked off a 13.02 at about 107 and they weren't even trying mind you.<P>I dont know what kind of truck you have Extreme but that Viper should easily walk you at 130mph.  Any truck is like pushing a brick through the wind.  If he only shifted to 5th at 130 then he wasn't in the proper gear.  He could have put it in 4th and left you bad.  Being a 98 that has 450 hp.  Perhaps he was playing with you?  I know that I play with cars that I know that are slower then me.  I give it just enough to keep them at my door to make them think they have a chance but never let them pass.  Perhaps he was doing that with you to.  I don't know I wasn't there so I am just throwing out possibilities.<P>Oh yea back the the original thread.  If you have a V8 cranking out about 300-350 hp will hang with a Viper till about 100 or so but I think after that the Viper just has more top end.  Plus the Viper can go 180 if it wants where I don't think a Fiero with the stock suspension will let it go that fast.  450 hp in a Fiero will toast any viper with a good driver.  Hennesey Viper would be close.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>2.8 With nitrous<BR>Soon to have a 3.2 with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Seanh||11-23-2000||10:50 PM||||Archies Purple-Pavment-Eater could probally waste a Viper any day of the week.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Zorba||11-23-2000||11:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>Archies Purple-Pavment-Eater could probally waste a Viper any day of the week.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Power to weight ratio would dictate a win for the v8 fiero. BUT...suspension and tranny gearing would be critical. Not to knock the viper. I am sure they are nice cars. there are more and more of them out now. But prices aren't coming down, they are going up. But...if you drive a viper and get beat by a fiero...hmmm that could be embarrassing but I have a friend who owns a new viper coupe and his philosophy is:"there are cars that can beat me out there,sure, but they are no vipers." Ferrari owners seems to have the same concept that's why they don't race. To them, they are driving an expensive machine and they could care less if they get beat by a mustang 5.0.  For what i gather, they think nothing of the poor man's car, whether it;s faster or not.<BR>But I would rather have a real nice, showroom car such as archie's pavement eater, than a viper. I am sure viper's maintenance is not cheap.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/racer.gif"> <P>||||reg||6
Z||000017||Zorba||11-23-2000||11:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>Archies Purple-Pavment-Eater could probally waste a Viper any day of the week.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/warber13.jpg"> <P><B>This one would do the trick...</B><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000018||Will||11-23-2000||11:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>Archies Purple-Pavment-Eater could probally waste a Viper any day of the week.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That is an impressive car, but I am VERY skeptical of that 450 HP number he quotes. TPI's run out of breath at 5000 rpm or below. They have comparatively worse top end than the Fiero 2.8. That means that to make 450 HP that engine would have to be pushing 450-500 ftlbs of torque. To get that kind of torque out of a 355, it would have to run 14:1 compression on leaded race gas. And it would ABSOLUTELY SHRED that Getrag in short order.<BR>     The only way that engine could be streetable and put out 450 HP is with forced induction or a 150+ shot of Nitrous, neither of which is mentioned in the description.<BR>     Come on, Archie, prove me wrong.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Black88GT||11-24-2000||12:01 AM||||Shaun, I always thought Vipers could only be had with the 6 speed manual?  I don't know if an auto tranny could handle that much power.<P>I think it would take a lot of money to make a Fiero compete with a Viper in all forms of competition.  Even if you could beat him to 60 he could still take you over 100. Or, even if you could take him in the corners he could still get you in the straights.  I would say around 40 grand but that is an extreme ballpark figure.  If you just wanted to drag in the Qtr., I think a heavily modified V8 Fiero would have a chance with a good driver.  However, I still don't think I would trust any Fiero tranny with 450 hp...<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">       <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Will||11-24-2000||09:25 AM||||There is an outfit in Britain called Stealth Racing that does six speed conversions on VW VR6 transaxles. Their full gearset kit is supposed to handle 400++ ftlbs. It *only* costs 2000 pounds, and there may be some length problems fitting it into a Fiero, but that is the best option I have come across for transmissions.<BR>     Learn more at <A HREF="http://www.stealthracing.co.uk." TARGET=_blank>www.stealthracing.co.uk.</A> <BR>     Quaife also makes a kit good for 300+ ftlbs: <A HREF="http://www.quaifeamerica.com" TARGET=_blank>www.quaifeamerica.com</A> <P>Doing the work yourself, I think a Fiero could compete with a Viper for 15-17 grand. That includes an IMSA body kit and 275/40-17 tires front and 335/35-17 tires rear.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 11-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||BBBAD GT||11-24-2000||11:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> That is an impressive car, but I am VERY skeptical of that 450 HP number he quotes. TPI's run out of breath at 5000 rpm or below. <BR>     Come on, Archie, prove me wrong.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I agree 100%, the TPI is a good street injection unit, not that great for high HP because its THE restriction.  Somewhere I saw the ZZ4 motor (Nytmare I think) dyno his motor with TPI and it pulled 260 rwhp.  Not exactly the 310-320 rwhp it would have to make to meet its 350+ flywheel horsepower rating, although that rating is with a carb.  It still proves the point about TPI.<P>I'm pretty sure the Fiero chassis wasn't designed to handle high HP, and this was confirmed by Chris who had a hot 350 (350+ hp) in the Fiero before the mild one went in.  He said the car was just scarey in its behavior with big HP.  It does just fine with 207 rwhp tho  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">    <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000022||saleenfiero||11-24-2000||12:23 PM||||There have been only two or three auto vipers made OEM, and they would be as easy to come by as an '89 fiero.  I've heard of people changing the trannys aftermarket though, that ugly ass Le-Ann Rimes did this case in point.  Lots of autos can handle the torque the 8.0 would hammer out, race tranny or not.  The new 911 turbo's got the 5 speed slushbox and is like 0.1 second of the six speed's 0-60.<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Fieroboy1||11-24-2000||05:40 PM||||Viper vs. Fiero???  I love these little cars too but unless we are talking straight line acceleration between stoplights who are we kidding?  I wouldn't take a fiero over 130 ever unless the suspension was totally redone and proper channeling of the air was taken care of.  A v/8 fiero with 4.10's and like 400 hp should hang with a viper between lights and that's because of gearing.  You'll run out of steam WAYYYYY before the viper does.  These little cars just aren't made for serious HP.  My Uncle's car which is on Archie's page listed under Pat Ciarcia's ultimate NHRA fiero is sitting in my uncle's garage because he couldn't put the power to the pavement consistently.  He ripped the freaking motor mount through the cradle steel with his 800 hp beast.  He now races a totally race vega because he gave up on the fiero. Love these cars for what they are and leave the vipers to the big boys.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Zorba||11-25-2000||01:57 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> He now races a totally race vega because he gave up on the fiero. Love these cars for what they are and leave the vipers to the big boys.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You do have a point. But 800hp in a fiero is a bit too much, don't you think? Let me just say that if he was using the stock fiero mount (angled iron, welded in with the archie kit) it is no wonder he ripped that to pieces. my question to you is: did he re-enforce that area of the kit? If not, he is to blame. Secondly, what tranny was he using to handle such monstrous torque? 500 ft pounds? more?<BR>You are right about suspension modifications, air intake, etc. But, for the record, I have SEEN VW bugs ripping vipers apart at the track! yes, the best time I have seen a vw bug do was 10.5 in the 1/4 mile!  I don't know what they put in those things to make them go that fast but I KNEW it was no V8 in there but some sort of modifed vw engine. I would not call these vw bugs "big boys" would you? So if they can do it, meaning the vw bug guys, why can't a fiero run at least 11's and beat the vipers in their magazine advertised 12.2-12.9 sec runs?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/clyde.jpg"> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000025||Zorba||11-25-2000||02:25 AM||||<B>Big boys and fieros</B><P>I think the fiero has a lot of potential as a viper killer but it needs to be modified to meet the challenge. Not to compare, but I think the VW bug, being rear engined and light weight is able to do wonders without a v8 engine in it. For the record, here are some track times, which beat even the 650 modified vipers:<BR>PRO CLASS RECORDS   <P><B>PRO-STOCK<P>  Domminick Luppino 9.821 ET<BR>  Ron Lummus 134.62 MPH<P>      PRO-TURBO<P>  Chris Bubetz 8.890 ET<BR>  Chris Bubetz 151.930 MPH<P> <BR> <BR>SUPER-STREET<P>  Dave Perkins 10.145 ET<BR>  Rafael Vasquez 130.57 MPH<BR></B><BR>There are pretty good numbers for a car that usually costs much less than a fiero. I am yet to see any viper drag strip numbers like the above.<BR> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000026||Will||11-25-2000||08:28 AM||||The point of hot-rodding is to spend little to have a car that's fast. The VW bug epitomises the "spend little" part, while any hot-rodder with good engineering skills can supply the "fast".<BR>     The Fiero is also a "spend little" platform, although not as much so as the bug. The engineering skill is much harder to come by.<P>Aside from that, lets do some power to weight figuring. Viper: 450 hp, 3300 lbs -&gt; 7.33 lbs/hp (that's damn good).<BR>Fiero: 2800 lbs, 400 hp -&gt; 7 lbs/hp. To keep the weight down, a Northstar should be used. With headwork and cams from <A HREF="http://www.chrfab.com," TARGET=_blank>www.chrfab.com,</A>  it should make 400 hp.<BR>     It should be obvious that tripling the stock power will require the need to reinforce suspension and chassis components I would prefer to run an '88 because of the trailing arms, but I would certainly use rod ends and no rubber bushings, in addition to reinforcing the engine/transaxle mounts, triangulating the cradle, possibly reinforcing the front cradle mounts, and bead-welding some of the factory pinch welds on the chassis.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000027||BBBAD GT||11-25-2000||08:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Zorba:<BR><B>[b]Big boys and fieros</B><P>I think the fiero has a lot of potential as a viper killer but it needs to be modified to meet the challenge. Not to compare, but I think the VW bug, being rear engined and light weight is able to do wonders without a v8 engine in it. For the record, here are some track times, which beat even the 650 modified vipers:<BR>PRO CLASS RECORDS   <P><B>PRO-STOCK<P>  Domminick Luppino 9.821 ET<BR>  Ron Lummus 134.62 MPH<P>      PRO-TURBO<P>  Chris Bubetz 8.890 ET<BR>  Chris Bubetz 151.930 MPH<P> <BR> <BR>SUPER-STREET<P>  Dave Perkins 10.145 ET<BR>  Rafael Vasquez 130.57 MPH<BR></B><BR>There are pretty good numbers for a car that usually costs much less than a fiero. I am yet to see any viper drag strip numbers like the above.<BR> <P>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>You are comparing apples to oranges here.  The cars you specified above are pure drag cars, that can't sit at a traffic light for no more than 10 minutes before overheating, and couldn't take a turn to save its life (last time I checked, the drag strip didn't have any turns).  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000028||KnightRyder31||11-25-2000||01:21 PM||||1993 Dodge Viper RT/10<BR>Price-$54,860<BR>Engine type-V10<BR>HP-400<BR>0-60--4.8<BR>1/4 mile- 13.1<BR>Top speed-165MPH<BR>60-0-156ft<BR>80-0-261ft<BR>Skidpad, g-0.96<BR>Slalom, MPH-62.7MPH<BR>MPG-14.0<P>These are official number's from Chrysler(My grandfather works for them, he gave me a info book for Chrysler auto's.<P>A hoped up Fiero could beat though's times.<BR>I guess it would all depend on the type of Viper; the GTS-R and the Vennom are of course another story...because they are rated for over 400HP. But the RT/10 isn't the fastest car in the world, and is very beatable. A 2001 Corvette could beat the RT/10. A 13 sec. 1/4 mile time is a good time, but I woulden't pay 55K for it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000029||Zorba||11-25-2000||01:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BBBAD GT:<BR><B> <BR>You are comparing apples to oranges here.  The cars you specified above are pure drag cars,  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I guess I will go out and buy me a viper then, since I have already been approved to finance one at $799 per month with 0 down.<BR>We are talking about 1/4 mile runs not turns and suspension performance. For turns, I will take the Acura NSX anytime. have you driven one? Or even the 90-96 300zx twin turbo or the old fashion 308 magnum pi ferrari (selling for $25,000_$35,000 these days). Have you driven one? I have driven all of the cars above and if it were for suspension and turns, twisty roads, etc. I would buy the above cars which don't have near the hp of a newer viper but are outstanding suspension cars. My point is that if a bug can run 8's at 1/4 mile, a fiero should be able to do 11's and beat the vipers. If we are talking handling performance plus speed, then aside from the viper, there are many other sports cars, including bmw's which can outrank the vipers.<BR>IF I wanted a viper, I would have sitting in my garage right now, with high monthly payments but it is so much more fun to have a v8 fiero or the northstar conversion that can kick butt without spending $45K-$70K for a highly publicized car. You see, hot rodding magazines are paid to embelish and promote cars. that's their job. Do you think their readers want to know about an 10 second fiero? <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000030||BBBAD GT||11-25-2000||05:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Zorba:<BR><B> <BR>I guess I will go out and buy me a viper then, since I have already been approved to finance one at $799 per month with 0 down.<BR>We are talking about 1/4 mile runs not turns and suspension performance. For turns, I will take the Acura NSX anytime. have you driven one? Or even the 90-96 300zx twin turbo or the old fashion 308 magnum pi ferrari (selling for $25,000_$35,000 these days). Have you driven one? I have driven all of the cars above and if it were for suspension and turns, twisty roads, etc. I would buy the above cars which don't have near the hp of a newer viper but are outstanding suspension cars. My point is that if a bug can run 8's at 1/4 mile, a fiero should be able to do 11's and beat the vipers. If we are talking handling performance plus speed, then aside from the viper, there are many other sports cars, including bmw's which can outrank the vipers.<BR>IF I wanted a viper, I would have sitting in my garage right now, with high monthly payments but it is so much more fun to have a v8 fiero or the northstar conversion that can kick butt without spending $45K-$70K for a highly publicized car. You see, hot rodding magazines are paid to embelish and promote cars. that's their job. Do you think their readers want to know about an 10 second fiero? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Mario<P>You missed the whole point I was tring to make.  You can't start comparing strip bugs to Vipers to V8 Fieros.  You need to start hanging out at the dragstrip to see how these cars are going fast.  I bet that bug doesn't have its original suspension, or anything original cept the sheetmetal.  <P>If you wanted a 'kick-butt' car, then you shouldn't of gone the V8 Fiero route.  There are many differnt paths you could of taken that are considerably less expensive than the V8 Fiero.  But, its your choice to do the Fiero thing.  <P>My most recent Stang mag tested a moded Viper with 580 rwhp that has gone 11.21 @ 131mph (the blown 5.0 with 548 rwhp went 10.35 @ 134mph).  You might think the 11.21 is slow, but you have to look at the mph.  That Viper is low 10 second 'capable', he's just not getting all of that HP to the ground.  <P>By all means go out and buy a Viper, then you can compare the two  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000031||Archie||11-25-2000||07:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>      Come on, Archie, prove me wrong.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't feel I have to prove anything, but I'll be happy to tell you just how wrong you are.<P>First off, I'd like to state that I resist becoming involved in these "such&such will beat such&such" threads. IMHO, These types of threads are a waste of time & I don't even look at them, but you decided you needed me involved in this and you sent me a private EMail to get me to look at this thread.<P>Now as far as proving you wrong, I don't really have to. The engine in the car you are questioning was built by the late Rich Warber. If you knew Rich or knew anything about him you wuldn't question his word that the car made 450hp. If you ever could drive the car you wouldn't question it's power either. I know that your post is aimed at reaching out "to put the touch on Archie", but this time you picked the wrong subject. Because I didn't build this engine, Your questioning the output of this car is directed at Rich Warber who is not here to argue with you himself. If Rich was here, and if I know him like I think I do, His reaction to your comments would be to grin and challenge you to bring out something to beat it. If you go to the page where the car is listed for sale <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/warberfs.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/warberfs.htm</A>  you would see that the car is owned by Rich's widow and she is the one selling it. Rich died Aug. 16, 1999, If you were a member of NIFE and had access to the Nov/Dec 1999 issue of Fiero Focus, you would see that the entire issue was dedicated to Rich. In that issue is a detailed list of items that were used in that 450hp engine & a Bio on Rich himself. I'm not going to list out the "laundry list" of items Rich put into that engine but I can tell you a couple of things...... that engine has 11.5:1 compression and a very highly modified injection system. I guarantee you that this TPI engine doesn't run out of breath all the way to 7 grand. I've personally witnessed the car running consistant 0 to 60 times of 3.8 sec. with 2 people in the car!<P>2 things for you to consider......<P>1) Your doubting of Rich's HP claims for his engine, is an insult to one of the top 5 cult heros of the entire Fiero community. Just ask around, EVERYONE knows this car and it's late owner. (except you  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>2) You are the 1st one I've ever heard of who has questioned this car. This car has won more than 25 1st. place awards in Fiero shows all across the country. There never has been a negative word said about this car or the guy that built it. You obviously have never seen this car or you would have had no doubts.<P>I'll personally vouch for this car and I'll tell you for sure that it will beat any Vette or Viper  0 to 100 mph. After 100 mph, I couldn't care if you guys jabber about it for a month.<P>ALSO to Zorba......<P>Now do you understand why I don't participate in these types of threads? <P>Archie<P><BR>||||reg||12
Z||000032||Zorba||11-26-2000||03:07 PM||||The challenge to Archie was uncalled for. But I  must agree with him and the performance of his "pavement eater" - he would not call it that for no reason!<P>The problem with "magazine" tests is that they are designed to "sell cars" and inform potential buyers about how fast,safe, etc, a  particular model is. As for hanging out at the track more often, I have been going to the race track for 4 years now and have drag raced numerous times.<P>I have personally witnessed "so called fast cars" such as modified honda civics, talons, 300zx (one of my own) camaros, mustangs, vipers, corvettes, new model firebirds, etc...get their butts kicked at the 1/4 mile by street legal VW Bugs, Vegas, Pintos and other modified cars. The magazines want you to believe that when you shell out $100,000 for a  sports car, that you are driving the "ultimate driving machine". Experienced racers laugh at this kind of stuff. One guy I knew who just bought his NEW viper went to the track with the idea he was going to kick butt. Well, if you call high 13's and low 14's "butt kicking" that's what he did. He ran three times and was disappointed and went home. He was running camaro runs. So much for a $70,000 car.<BR>Sure there are things to consider such as looks, handling, street legal issues, etc.<BR>But, in my opinion, I would rather have the $20,000 pavement eater than the $75,000 good looks of a viper running high 12's, mid 13's. It's just too much money (for me anyway) for the end result performance. I have witnessed the same thing with Ferrari's, Acura NSX, Nissans, Hondas, etc. Sure they are fast cars for the average guy who is used to driving his buick skylark or honda, but in real track times and performance, they don't even come close. <P>They are fast cars when compared to "stock, mass produced" vehicles.<P>I guess what I am trying to say is this, you can shell out $75,000 for a viper (finance it, whatever) but you won't be driving a "super car" as they want you to believe you are. ANother case here, a local bodybuilder has a yellow viper. He got beat by a modified camaro with 383 (stroked) TPI owned by a guy I know at Pep Boys. So. His car cost him $6,000.<BR>Anyway...I can see why these threads can become "pointless"  - I just wanted to know if anyone had beaten a viper with a modified v8 fiero and I guess Archie's statement of beating any viper 0-100mph says it all. You need his pavement eater to beat the viper.<BR>I personally think the viper is a very good looking car and I have nothing against it. Wouldn't mind owning one either. BUT...I think (IT) like other sports cars (are overrated) to get people to buy them. Some attorney, doctor guy who wants a "super fast" car and reads the magazines these cars are advertised in. I would have a grin on my face driving the "pavement eater" and smoking any viper or new corvette that challenged me for a race - safely of course.<BR>I must also agree with Archie that, after 100mph who cares? IF he can beat a viper 0-60 in less than 1/4 mile, that is good enough for me. Anyway...no offense intended to anyone here. My respects to the man who built the engine for the pavement eater.<BR>For those of you, who have money, and live near Archie's shop, go take a look at the car. (pavement eater)...||||reg||1
Z||000033||Phil||11-26-2000||03:28 PM||||No matter what you drive there is always somebody out there that can beat you. Even John Force gets beaten at times.||||reg||1
Z||000034||BBBAD GT||11-26-2000||06:42 PM||||I agree mostly what you are saying about building a car for lot less $$$ and embarrising more expensive 'super cars', thats what motivated me to build up my 5.0, to kick any butt that comes along.  <P>But, if you start comparing quarter mile times, you have to take it with a grain of salt.  The car is only as fast as the driver.  The attorneys/doctors that buy these Vipers most likely couldn't drive it at the strip to its fullest potential.  A 13/12/11 second <B>CAR</B> needs a 13/12/11 second <B>DRIVER</B>.  The MPH on your timeslip shows the HP, and the ET is a function of how well you use the HP (and the driver has alot to do with it).  <P>If you still have doubts about the Viper, then you should start challenging them to a dyno run.  This takes the driver error out of the equation.  I've seen the early Viper put out 400 rwhp bone stock, which is pretty darn good for being off the showroom floor.  That alone would get my vote for a 'super car'.    ||||reg||1
Z||000035||Zorba||11-27-2000||10:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> The car is only as fast as the driver.  The attorneys/doctors that buy these Vipers most likely couldn't drive it at the strip to its fullest potential.  <BR>If you still have doubts about the [/B]<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I agree with the above statement.<P>Additionally, if you have plenty of cash you can have both. A nicely set up fiero or other "modified" car and maybe a viper or other "super car" for the weekend as well.<BR>I've learned, thru experience, that, it's best to have a fast car that's paid for than an alledged 'super car' with huge monthly payments. Now I am sticking with what I can afford! It's more fun this way. <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000036||RiceCooker||11-27-2000||08:22 PM||||I'll tell you what, if I was 5 or 10 years older, I would drive down to Illinois walk right into Archie's shop, look him straight in the eye and say........<P>"One Purple Pavement Eater to go please"<P>no kidding, First of all $20,000 is a great price. Think of new cars, what do you get for that much, a crappy four cylinder family sedan or maybe a little better. Buying a high performance machine with only 20thousand or so miles on the engine is like a new car, in some ways. <BR>Does anyone else think that thing looks way better than a Viper anyway? I do! More Intimidating and also people would think that you were lying if you told them it was a fiero.<BR>I could go on and on about why that car kicks a$$ but i might offend someone.<BR>So that's just my $0.031 (Canadian Currency)<P>If it eats pavement, what does it $hit?<BR>Anyone who wants to race it.||||reg||1
Z||000037||BBBAD GT||11-27-2000||08:43 PM||||Has the Purple Pavement Eater ever been down the 1/4 mi?<P>No flames, no yelling, no digs, just curious.||||reg||1
Z||000038||Pontiaddict||11-27-2000||09:30 PM||||Yeah, you can get one Purple Pavement Eater for the price of 3 Viper hoods. (Really, They cost 7 grand a piece!)<P>Now that's what I call a value. (Uhmm. Not the Viper)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000039||Fiero5||11-27-2000||10:23 PM||||I have met Archie, and he is a straight shooter. I believe what he says about that Pavement eater. That would be one awesome Fiero to own and drive. Wow!<P>On a lighter note, anyone who wants to see one of my Fiero 4 bangers take on a Viper, go to: <A HREF="http://www.driveway.com/share?sid=fd87d014.ed5d6&name=Lycos+Network&view=1" TARGET=_blank>http://www.driveway.com/share?sid=fd87d014.ed5d6&name=Lycos+Network&view=1</A> <P>There are some other very cool Fiero videos there also.||||reg||1
Z||000040||Zorba||11-28-2000||11:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RiceCooker:<BR><B>I'll tell you what, if I was 5 or 10 years  .</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It's a great price for what it has to offer.<BR>I think the only reason it hasn't sold yet is because of "financing" - Traditional financing will still look at the car as a fiero and go by the blue book values not taking into account any of the mods. Sometimes, if you have a car appraised by two different certified appraisers, then a credit union may loan against it, but it's rare. Typically they do this with kitcars.<BR>IF there was financing on that car it would be gone. $20,000 is nothing for a car these days but you ca to take into consideration low interest rate financing as opposed to "pulling the money" out of savings for a car. You have to be well-to-do. Some people just have that kind of cash and think nothing of spending $20,000-$200,000 on a car. Locally, I know a guy who bought a "real" lamborghini diablo for $285,000. His age? 28. His job. Computers and internet stuff. How he paid for it? Cash! Isn't that something? Some folks just got the dough!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000041||BBBAD GT||11-28-2000||12:51 PM||||Also I'll add to that this PPE isn't (what I consider) a daily driver.  The high compression would require good good gas, and I bet the cam isn't exactly glass smooth either.  I'm not dogging the PPE, I'm just saying you have to give up some things to make that big HP naturally aspirated.  With that in mind, the person who would buy the PPE is probably a person who isn't looking to buy a new car, but a weekend toy that would collect trophies on the side.  This is common with tricked out Fieros, they become weekend cars that the owner wipes it with a diaper  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000042||Will||11-28-2000||05:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B>I don't feel I have to prove anything, but I'll be happy to tell you just how wrong you are.<P>First off, I'd like to state that I resist becoming involved in these "such&such will beat such&such" threads. IMHO, These types of threads are a waste of time & I don't even look at them, but you decided you needed me involved in this and you sent me a private EMail to get me to look at this thread.<P>&lt;snip&gt;<P>ALSO to Zorba......<P>Now do you understand why I don't participate in these types of threads? <P>Archie<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I don't know if you took my psot as having attitude or not, but it was not meant that way. I have a few issues to address in your post.<P>*I* did not sent you a private e-mail. Get your facts straight before you get into a huff.<P>I am not a member of NIFE. Not living in Northern Illinois, I have no reason to be. It is therefore unreasonable to expect me to know who Rich Warber is. Aside from that, it is unreasonable to expect, as you seem to, that I am a member of NIFE.<BR>     Yes, if I had driven the car I would certainly know how powerful it is. You are quite the master of the obvious. You don't seem to be a master of logic, however. If you don't have to prove me wrong, what are you going to do? Let my statement stand? You don't do that either. You could have saved a lot of breath by giving me the straight dope without the attitude.<BR>     As for <B>I know that your post is aimed at reaching out "to put the touch on Archie"</B>. You seem to be a never-ending source of things that make me go "Hmmmm...." today. I definitely must have posted what I did to try to "put the touch on Archie". This must be plain as day to you because you certainly know me better than I know myself.<BR>     I bet you don't participate in threads like this because there is a greater likelihood of finding people in such threads who don't say "We're not worthy", and your ego just can't take the lack of praise. Do you not like it when I assume I know your motives? Don't assume you know mine.<BR>     Your assertion that I am insulting Mr. Warber by questioning a apparently extravagant claim for an engine he built has got to be one of the greatest Non-Sequitur's of all time. There are several billion people in the world who have never heard of Rich Warber (or V8 Archie and the magnificent Purple Pavement Eater, for that matter). Would you accuse all of them of insulting him if they questioned the claim I questioned? Especially if only given the information available on your website.<BR>     I don't care about or follow car shows. I don't care about IRM rocker panels. I don't care about extended wingtips. I don't care about Deep Amethyst Pearl.<BR>     If you've never heard a negative word said about the car, then you haven't been talking to the right people. I look for negative opinions; they help me refine my ideas.<BR>     Here are a few negative words for you: I don't like 3" chopped tops. Doesn't Lexan get scratched rather easily? I don't like raised wings that interfere with rear visibility. I don't like decklid scoops that are not supported by aerodynamic data and also interfere with rear visibility. I don't like anything that is done for looks or style that has no impact on performance.<BR>     Don't you like new experiences?<BR>     Have you raced every Viper and Vette in existence with this car? Look in the latest issue of Popular Hot Rodding. Look at the work of Lingenfelter. I don't think this car would stand a chance against a 415 ci LT5.<P>     I have no beef with the car, or with Rich Warber. I simply want the truth, whether it favor me or make me look stupid, to be proclaimed from the rooftops.<P><BR>P.S. My condolences to Mrs. Warber for her loss.<P>P.P.S. When are you going to put an LT5 into a Fiero?||||reg||1
Z||000043||Will||11-28-2000||05:24 PM||||Now a few questions about the car: What have you done to the chassis the handle all that power?<BR>Has it ever been down the quarter mile?<BR>What size tires does it wear?<BR>How well does it handle? Does it have a rear swaybar?<BR>Does it need 94 Octane or can it run with 92?<BR>What kind of cylinder heads does it have?<P>To wind to 7k the only thing that system could have in common with a stock TPI is runner length. The runners themselves and the manifold base would have to be oversized, as would the injectors and throttle body.<P>Why dont you post some of this info on your website, so that you don't get uppity MoFo's such as myself questioning the mighty Archie?||||reg||1
Z||000044||Pontiaddict||11-28-2000||07:04 PM||||Will Nobody said the fuel injection was stock. As a matter if fact, Archies website said it was from Street & Performance. Not only do they make larger runners, lower intake and throttle body. But they also make custom wiring harnesses and sell alrger injectors and custom proms. <P>And as for Rich Warber, as far as I could tell, Archie was stating that if he put that much effort into his car, then the engine would have had just as much custom work done to it.<P>As for the Lt5, It weighs nearly a ton. (figuratively speaking) Just what you need in the back of a Fiero. and it's so huge that it barely fit into an Impala SS engine bay (Tim Allens Binford 6100) without major modification to the car.<P>If I remember correctly, Lingenfelter's fastest 'Vette was an LS1<P>Archie, Will's questions (the ones after the rant) were valid ones. If you have the info, we'd all like to know.||||reg||1
Z||000045||KnightRyder31||11-28-2000||08:36 PM||||DAMN WILL!!!||||reg||1
Z||000046||Northstar308||11-29-2000||01:08 AM||||My fiero (northstar) isn't nearly as radical as the purple pavement eater, but I wouldn't hesitate putting it up to a viper.  Right now, it is geared high (ie, 75mph in 1st gear) and past 40mph, the viper doesn't have a shot (all the way to as fast as you dare drive, 150+); low end, it would take it easy, but change the gearing if you are looking for 1/4 times.  All that, and it's plenty reliable and still gets 30mpg on the highway.  Beat that.||||reg||1
Z||000047||BBBAD GT||11-29-2000||08:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Northstar308:<BR><B> ... the viper doesn't have a shot .... Beat that.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Any 1/4 mi timeslips or dyno numbers to back up that claim?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000048||Will||11-29-2000||12:09 PM||||Here's why I brought the issue up in the first place:<P>     All the "oversized" TPI runners I have ever seen have been siamesed. Instead of two round tubes next to each other, the runner consists of one oblong/rectangular tube. Upon perusing the Street & Performance website, I see that everything there listed as "large runner" has siamesed runners.<BR>     The PPE does NOT have siamesed runners. My reaction was to assume that it was using stock diameter runners. From the picture, the runners LOOK stock diameter. If there are non-siamesed oversized runners, I have never heard of them, although would be possible to build them. Probably more trouble than making siamesed runners, but possible.<BR>     I didn't realize that so few people here knew anything about TPI.<BR>     Does it have oversized runners or not?<P><BR>Pontiaddict:<P>     The LT5 has aluminum block and heads. Check out <A HREF="http://www.zr1.net" TARGET=_blank>www.zr1.net</A>  for info. I know it's larger than a SBC, but that's to be expected from a DOHC engine.<BR>     I didn't say his fastest Vette was an LT5, but 415 ci LT5's are damn fast. His fastest is an LS1 only because he hasn't turboed an LT5, at least not to my knowledge.<BR>     For info on 600 HP LT5 packages, check out <A HREF="http://www.lingenfelter.com/packages/pricing_zr1.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.lingenfelter.com/packages/pricing_zr1.htm</A> <BR>     Elsewhere on his site he also lists 725 HP, 700 ftlb Viper packages: <A HREF="http://www.lingenfelter.com/packages/viper540ci.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.lingenfelter.com/packages/viper540ci.htm</A> <BR>     Does the PPE really stand a chance against ANY Viper or Vette?<BR>     ||||reg||1
Z||000049||Will||11-29-2000||12:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Northstar308:<BR><B>My fiero (northstar) isn't nearly as radical as the purple pavement eater, but I wouldn't hesitate putting it up to a viper.  Right now, it is geared high (ie, 75mph in 1st gear) and past 40mph, the viper doesn't have a shot (all the way to as fast as you dare drive, 150+); low end, it would take it easy, but change the gearing if you are looking for 1/4 times.  All that, and it's plenty reliable and still gets 30mpg on the highway.  Beat that.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>     I guess you are using the 4T80E transmission? Did you do your conversion yourself?<BR>     What RPM does it turn at 75 mph?<BR>     I'm doing mine with a Getrag, but the fifth gear is really too short for such an engine: 2600 RPM at 75 mph.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000050||Will||11-29-2000||12:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KnightRyder31:<BR><B>DAMN WILL!!!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is that a curse or an exclamation?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000051||Zorba||11-29-2000||12:55 PM||||So much for the fiero verus viper question.<BR>It costs  $38,500.00 to turn a viper into a 750hp monster according to the link provided here. It probably costs $10,000 to make a fiero run into the 11's, plus the price of the car. If you add $38,500 plus another $70,000 for the viper, it better be a super car for that kind of cash. The more i read into this thread, the more I see it deviated from the original post. Now it's turned into a personal attack and ego battles. Surprisingly, no one here is either driving a stock viper or anything like the pavement eater and yet we all seem to know what's best and faster. <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/red.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000052||Will||11-29-2000||02:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Zorba:<BR><B>So much for the fiero verus viper question.<BR>It costs  $38,500.00 to turn a viper into a 750hp monster according to the link provided here. It probably costs $10,000 to make a fiero run into the 11's, plus the price of the car. If you add $38,500 plus another $70,000 for the viper, it better be a super car for that kind of cash. The more i read into this thread, the more I see it deviated from the original post. Now it's turned into a personal attack and ego battles. Surprisingly, no one here is either driving a stock viper or anything like the pavement eater and yet we all seem to know what's best and faster. <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/red.gif">  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Threads go off topic quite frequently; it happens quite a bit when someone asks a question that has an obvious answer, a question to which no one knows the answer, or a question about a non-issue.<BR>     When I posted this info about the Viper I was refuting Archie's rather brash statement that the PPE would outrun any Vette or Viper 0-100. The fallacies of that statement are as obvious as the answer to your question: Of course the Fiero is going to be more cost effective in a straight line than the Viper. That is a big part of Hot Rodding.<BR>     I admit that we've had ego on both sides of this "discussion", but where has anyone been attacked? This is the second time you've mentioned it and I still don't see where you're coming from. Like I told Archie, don't assume you know my motivations. Saying "I am skeptical of that claim" is not the same as saying "Archie's a lying SOB". Anyone who's still paying attention can go back and see that I said the former, not the latter.<BR>     Why is this thread in TD&Q anyway?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000053||Slammed Fiero||11-29-2000||04:01 PM||||I would be interested to see how a Northstar Fiero would stack up against a regular V8 car or a viper... A Northstar car weighs in the neighbourhood of 3100lbs , The fastest one I have Witnessed run the 1/4 mile in is  13.7 , this was Chris moores car at the pontiac nationals this past summer. He is currently having the car tuned By John Lingenfelter. I think John Normans claims of 11's and such are false. In the hot rod magazine he claims the car runs 11's before the bottle , then also goes on to say it's dynoed at 260 Rear wheel Hp.. I say impossible , the car is inheriently heavy , all Fiero's are. IF you work Chris Moores 1/4 mile time in his N* car into HP , it calculates into 346 Flywheel HP, which I think is believable with the absence of power steering and the addition of a free flow exhaust.. Unless he has the motor all built up ,I would think that Normans car is high 12's at best. The northstar lacks the Torque of a small block and certianally a Viper. The weight transfer of a V8 Fiero of any kind is killer , but not enough for a Northstar to make up for the Torque the Viper has.  There is another Northstar car here in Canada Running 13.8 in the 1/4 mile , it has some electrical issues to work out so it should be in the same ball park as the other cars (high 12's) . Why are we comparing to a Viper anyway?? I like the Viper , But from an engineering standpoint consider this:<P>In an average F1 engine, running at 18,000 rpm (300 revs per<BR>second), each spark plug fires 30 times a second. An n/a 3.0lt  V-10 F1<BR>engine produces about 280 hp/L on 97-octane unleaded pump gas. A Dodge<BR>Viper 8.0L V-10 produces only 56 hp/L and an IRL 3.5L V-8 about 185 hp/L<BR>on methanol.<BR>    At 17 or 18000 RPM those pistons change direction about 500 times<BR>per second.  They said the wrist pins undergo somewhere around 8000 Gs<BR>of force at that speed.<P><BR>I forget where I was going with this ...so many thoughts... I just want to see some hard evidence of a Northstar Fiero Pulling these claimed monster times.... 13.6 , very respectable for any car. Northstar is smooth and a rare conversion , thats why I'm considering it. who cares about Vipers? Big Boats ... raw times are what we should be looking at.... So lets find some websites with real Data... Don't trust magazines there all full of BS... Corvette Z06 1/4 mile in mid 12's ..The best I have seen one run is Low 13's.. GM selling the sizzle and not the steak.....<P>No archie doesn't have to prove a thing , we all know him and have seen his work in one form or another , But nothing says Lovin like a Fiero smoking a Claimed supercar... By the way ..Archie ..when are you updating the Webpage??? Put some 1/4 mile runs in video form on it ....<P>Jonathan McCreery ||||reg||1
Z||000054||BBBAD GT||11-29-2000||06:29 PM||||REALLY off topic, but if you want to see an incar video of a 11.13 @ 127 run, go here:<BR> <A HREF="http://home.att.net/~nomercy5.0/Quartermile.asf" TARGET=_blank>http://home.att.net/~nomercy5.0/Quartermile.asf</A> <P>Here is the timeslip:<BR> <A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/TimeSlips.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/TimeSlips.htm</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000055||Will||11-29-2000||07:28 PM||||To continue my trend of being highly opinionated in this thread, I would like to comment about Northstars, driveline loss, etc.<BR>     I don't understand why people say a N* is a low torque engine. The HO version has 300 HP and 295 ftlbs. SBC's with similar specific output would likely have higher power/torque ratios. Remember that a 300 HP SBC is in a considerably lower state of tune than the N*.<BR>     Also, there is no reason that a N* car MUST weigh 3100 lbs. A car using the 4T80E would weigh that much, but 200+ lbs can be cut by using a Getrag. My car weighs 2750 lbs. When I do the conversion, I will add the following weight: remote partial flow oil filter, partial flow coolant filter, oil/water heat exchanger (made of Monel), and what is necessary to relocate the battery up front. I do't expect weight to climb above 2850 at the highest.<BR>     Slammed Fiero: what driveline loss was used to calculate 346 fwhp?<BR>     I've heard quite a few people spout driveline loss numbers on this forum, but from what I've seen, no one seems to realize that different finds of drivelines have different levels of loss. A manual transaxle like the Getrag will have quite a low loss figure copared to an automatic longitudinal driveling as in an automatic Vette or Firebird. There are two factors to consider: an automatic transmission has higher loss than a manual, and a longitudinal driveline has a higher loss than a transverse driveline.<BR>     The automatic/manual difference shoudl be obvious, but the longitudinal/transverse difference is due to the hypoid bevel gear used in the differential.<BR>     In any gear in a Getrag, there is one transfer by spur gears in the transmission and one spur gear transfer in the final drive.<BR>     In a Borg-WarnerW T-56, for example, there are two spur gear transfers in 1,2,3,5,&6, in addition to a hypoid bevel gear transfer. In fourth the input shaft is locked to the output shaft, and the are no spur gear transfers in the transmission.<BR>     The hypoid bevel transfer involves considerably more friction than a regular bevel or spur gear transfer, and thus more loss.<BR>     While an automatic Firebird may have 15-20% driveline loss, a transverse manual like a Getrag is probably 10% or below. I don't know the numbers for sure, but it is something to consider.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000056||Slammed Fiero||11-29-2000||08:19 PM||||346 Fly Wheel Hp was the Number John Lingenfelter Gave Chris Moore Concerning His Northstar car This was not calculated withthe use of a Dyno , Chris's car should see Lingenfelters Dyno in the next month.. No the car doesn't have to weigh 3100lbs , But I think the majority of conversions out there are performed using the Stock Caddy tranny. The Northstar engine is much like the Zr1 motor and many other multi cam engines , it has a peakier torque curve. It reminded me of riding in a 911 , it all seems to hit you at 2500 RPM , Where as a small block has it all down low. I still haven't seen performance #'s and thats what I'm looking for..... Vipers are Fine and dandy , I know of a few Naturally aspirated 911's (3.6-3.8ltr) that will beat a viper in the 1/4 , I want hard Facts ....not "I think.." I have ridden in a northstar fiero on a few occasions and was impressed , But how fast will one thats properly tuned really go???<P>also see<BR> <A HREF="http://www.chrfab.com" TARGET=_blank>www.chrfab.com</A>  <P>Northstar performance||||reg||1
Z||000057||Northstar308||11-29-2000||09:58 PM||||No timeslips for the 1/4 to back up a viper claim (actually, my car is geared such that I would dramatically lose in such a contest).  However, like I said, once you have the car revved up, it is pretty exotic and I think it would be a genuine battle in speeds of 50-150.  <BR>   The reason people claim the N* has little torque is becuase it feels that way.  The N* may have 295 lbs, but it is torque phased such that the curve comes in much higher than would be in a typical rear wheel drive car (this is actually desireable for the fiero however, as it preserves/encourages great handling).  With a 4.xx rear end (like John Norman's), it should be able to run 0-60's in about 4.8-5 secs, but being that mine is geared with a 2.xx, that's not happening (ACE Conversions built my car, DURANGO, CO).  However, this gearing gives me good economy and plenty of enjoyment once it's revved up.||||reg||1
