Z||000060||Pontiaddict||11-30-2000||05:14 PM||||Maybe the runners are extrude honed. <P>I know the LT5 is aluminum, the fact that it's still heavy is my point. (and pretty sad) I still can't find any place with exact numbers but heavier than an all iron small block would be a good guess. <P>Besides the glory of owning a ZR1 Fiero, I don't see the point in building one. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000061||Will||11-30-2000||06:49 PM||||TPI runners are made of thin wall aluminum tubing. There's no material there that can be extrude honed.<P>     How much would extrude honing affect the performance of the stock Fiero intake system/manifolds?||||reg||1
Z||000062||Pontiaddict||12-01-2000||05:14 PM||||Extrude honing ought to smooth the curves and transitions in the Fiero intake.<BR>I'm not sure if the 2.8 needs the extra runner volume but it could be a good thing for a 3.4 swap.<P>On another note.<BR>These are some chassis dyno figures from the may 1998 Hot Rod:<P>'98 Caddilac Eldorado ETC with 2731 miles<BR>rated power: 300 hp@6000; 295 lbft@4400<BR>dyno:216 hp@5800; 222 lbft@4400<P>'98 Viper GTS  with 2960 miles<BR>rated power: 450 hp@5200; 490 lbft@3700<BR>dyno: 406.7hp @5300; 445 lbft4200<P>'98 Grand Prix GTP with 2864 miles<BR>rated power: 240 hp@5200; 280 lbft@3200<BR>dyno: 197.2 hp@5600; 262 lbft@2900<P>I figured that would help in seeing diveline losses. ||||reg||1
